# Security headers (2026-07-23 audit): the directory UI renders relay-sourced
# strings client-side — CSP is the defense-in-depth backstop behind esc().
# connect-src allows self + the Nostr relays the browser module queries.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://bitcoineconomy.ai https://cloudflareinsights.com wss://nos.lol wss://relay.primal.net wss://relay.damus.io wss://nostr.bitcoiner.social; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; form-action 'self'

# Every text route served its bytes as UTF-8 and declared no charset, until
# 2026-08-09. With no charset a browser falls back to the locale default, so the
# spec pages rendered "â€"" for every em dash and "â‰ " for the ≠ in
# "announced ≠ curated" — on the one page we ask a stranger to read before
# listing a service. The files were never wrong; only the header was. It matters
# more on the agent surfaces than the human one: llms.txt and agents.txt are
# fetched by parsers that do not all guess, and a mojibake manifest is a
# mojibake instruction. Declared explicitly rather than left to the client.
# Scoped to *.md deliberately: a bare /spec/* also caught the two .schema.json
# files and served JSON Schema as text/markdown, at the very URL each schema
# names as its own $id. Keep the glob (not two hardcoded paths) so a future
# spec .md still gets its charset.
/spec/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
/entries/*
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
/agents.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
/robots.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Agent-readable routes are fetched cross-origin by tools and crawlers.
# Four routes were missing from this list until 2026-08-07 and served no CORS header
# while every sibling did: /master.json, /agents.txt, /l402index.json, /l402space.json.
# They are not obscure — llms.txt names /master.json as the static fallback for the
# route it heads "Start here: one fetch", and the two l402 files as the tier fallbacks.
# So a browser-resident agent following the documented cold-KV path was blocked by
# CORS, which is the exact failure the fallbacks exist to prevent.
/master.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/master-version.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/agents.txt
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/l402index.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/l402space.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/directory.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/tools.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/snapshot.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/models.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/llms.txt
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/entries/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/openapi.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/.well-known/ai-plugin.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/spec/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/uptime.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
/anchors/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
